    <header class="topbar"><a class="brand" href="/">ER Practice Guide <span>Conduct &amp; performance</span></a><nav class="quick-links"><a href="/pathways">Pathways</a><a href="/glossary">Glossary</a></nav></header>
    <main class="standalone">
      <p class="eyebrow">Orientation</p>
      <h1>Start here</h1>
      <p class="lead">A decision map for framing a conduct or performance matter. It is a way to think, not a mandatory employer process.</p>
      <div class="notice">Training material only — not legal advice. Check the current legislation, applicable industrial instruments, contract, policy, evidence and case-specific advice before relying on any conclusion.</div>

      <h2>1. Name the presenting issue</h2>
      <p>Start with the thing that brought the matter to attention, without deciding too early what legal category it belongs to.</p>
      <ul class="principles compact">
        <li>Conduct: what is alleged to have happened?</li>
        <li>Performance: what standard was expected, and what fell short?</li>
        <li>Capacity or attendance: is the issue ability, absence, leave or communication?</li>
        <li>Complaint or relationship issue: who has raised what concern?</li>
        <li>Safety or wellbeing: is there an immediate risk requiring attention first?</li>
        <li>Mixed matter: are several of these present at once?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>2. Separate facts, allegations and decisions</h2>
      <div class="key"><h3>Ask three different questions</h3><ol><li>What is known or evidenced?</li><li>What is alleged but contested?</li><li>What decision, if any, has already been made?</li></ol><p>A pre-existing decision can change the legal analysis. Do not describe a proposed outcome as if it were already settled, and do not treat an allegation as a finding.</p></div>

      <h2>3. Check for immediate issues</h2>
      <p>Before analysing sanction or dismissal, identify anything that may require immediate action or a different source of advice:</p>
      <ul class="principles compact"><li>Immediate safety, medical or welfare risk.</li><li>Evidence that may be lost, altered or inaccessible.</li><li>Possible workers compensation, protected leave or incapacity.</li><li>Privacy, surveillance or sensitive medical information.</li><li>A complaint, inquiry, union activity or other potentially protected conduct.</li></ul>

      <h2>4. Identify the sources that may control</h2>
      <p>Do not assume the Fair Work Act is the only source. Check which of these may matter:</p>
      <div class="link-grid"><a href="/modules/agreements-and-policies"><b>Agreement or policy</b><small>What procedure, standard or obligation does it actually create?</small></a><a href="/modules/general-protections"><b>General protections</b><small>Could the reason be connected to a workplace right or protected activity?</small></a><a href="/modules/medical-capacity"><b>Capacity and adjustment</b><small>Could medical information, disability or adjustment questions change the analysis?</small></a><a href="/modules/safety-breaches"><b>Safety and WHS</b><small>Is the workplace issue separate from the disciplinary question?</small></a></div>

      <h2>5. Map parallel pathways</h2>
      <p>A conduct or performance matter may engage more than one pathway. Use the <a href="/pathways">parallel pathway map</a> before narrowing the issue to unfair dismissal.</p>

      <h2>6. Decide what is still uncertain</h2>
      <p>Write down the unanswered questions. A useful next step is usually the one that resolves the most important uncertainty, not the one that moves the matter fastest toward a sanction.</p>
      <div class="key"><h3>Before moving to a decision</h3><ul><li>Can the employee understand the substance of the concern?</li><li>What contrary explanation or evidence remains?</li><li>What source governs the standard or process?</li><li>Which parts are legal requirements, factual assessments or local choices?</li><li>What non-dismissal outcomes remain open?</li></ul></div>

      <p class="backlink"><a href="/">← Return to the guide</a> · <a href="/glossary">Open the glossary</a> · <a href="/pathways">Open the pathway map</a></p>
